WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Here are two clear liquids, cyclohexane and cyclohexene.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.120
Both have a ring of six carbons, and

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.120
they boil about two degrees apart.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.000
Shake each with orange bromine water.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.480
In one tube, the colour vanishes.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.440
In the other, the water goes pale too,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.160
but look closer: the orange has moved into

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.200
a layer on top.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.160
So how do you run this test fairly,

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.320
and what does the result really tell you?

00:00:28.334 --> 00:00:30.093
You've seen the chemistry in the last two

00:00:30.174 --> 00:00:30.973
videos.

00:00:31.214 --> 00:00:33.613
Bromine water gets its orange colour from the

00:00:33.694 --> 00:00:35.853
bromine molecules dissolved in it.

00:00:36.174 --> 00:00:38.573
Bromine adds across a carbon to carbon double

00:00:38.654 --> 00:00:40.973
bond, so the bromine is used up, and

00:00:41.054 --> 00:00:42.253
its colour goes.

00:00:42.574 --> 00:00:45.133
An alkane has no double bond to open,

00:00:45.214 --> 00:00:47.133
so in the dark, nothing adds.

00:00:47.694 --> 00:00:50.093
That makes these two a fair pair: the

00:00:50.174 --> 00:00:52.253
same ring of six carbons, and the only

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:54.573
real difference is one double bond.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.840
Now plan it so the result can only

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.840
come from that double bond.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:04.840
Use matched amounts: one millilitre of each hydrocarbon

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.040
into two millilitres of the same bromine water.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.520
Set up a third tube with one millilitre

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.560
of plain water instead, as a control: it's

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.040
diluted by the same amount, but there's nothing

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.120
in it for the bromine to react with.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.840
Stopper each tube, shake it the same way

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.080
for ten seconds, and stand it against a

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.520
white tile, so a faint colour shows.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.840
Work in normal room light, away from sunlight,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.240
because bright light can start a slow reaction

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.200
with an alkane.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.640
Cyclohexene first.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.560
The orange disappears within seconds, leaving two colourless

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:40.400
layers.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:43.320
The double bond opens and bromine adds across

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:45.160
it, so the bromine is used up.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.440
One possible product is one, two-dibromocyclohexane, with

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.520
a bromine atom on each carbon of the

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:53.640
old double bond.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:57.160
In water, other colourless addition products can form

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.560
too, so nothing is left to give colour.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.080
With bromine in an organic solvent instead, you

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.840
get just the dibromo compound: two reactants, one

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:07.640
product.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:08.920
That's addition.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:13.973
Now cyclohexane, and here's the trap.

00:02:14.454 --> 00:02:17.893
Cyclohexane doesn't mix with water, so it floats

00:02:17.934 --> 00:02:18.773
as a top layer.

00:02:19.414 --> 00:02:22.693
Bromine is non-polar, so it dissolves far better

00:02:22.734 --> 00:02:25.333
in cyclohexane than in water, and when you

00:02:25.414 --> 00:02:27.333
shake, most of it moves up.

00:02:27.894 --> 00:02:30.213
The water goes pale, but the top layer

00:02:30.294 --> 00:02:31.493
turns orange.

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:34.213
The colour has moved, not disappeared.

00:02:34.934 --> 00:02:36.053
Now look at the control.

00:02:36.534 --> 00:02:38.773
It's been diluted just as much, and it's

00:02:38.974 --> 00:02:40.293
still clearly orange.

00:02:40.774 --> 00:02:43.333
So dilution can't explain the pale water.

00:02:43.814 --> 00:02:46.053
The bromine is still there, in the top

00:02:46.134 --> 00:02:46.533
layer.

00:02:46.934 --> 00:02:47.893
No reaction.

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:52.220
So can an alkane react with bromine at

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:52.620
all?

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.820
Yes, with light, as in the last video.

00:02:56.060 --> 00:02:58.860
In a teacher demonstration, take that orange top

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.900
layer: it's bromine dissolved in cyclohexane.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.380
In the dark, it stays orange.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.740
Under a UV lamp, the colour slowly fades,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.820
and damp blue litmus paper at the mouth

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.260
of the tube turns red.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:13.180
A strip over a tube kept in the

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:16.300
dark changes far less, so the red isn't

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:17.580
just bromine vapour.

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:21.580
That's hydrogen bromide, an acidic gas, and it's

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:25.500
the evidence for substitution: cyclohexane and bromine give

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:28.220
bromocyclohexane and H Br.

00:03:30.234 --> 00:03:32.953
So what does a colourless result tell you?

00:03:33.034 --> 00:03:35.193
There's a carbon to carbon double or triple

00:03:35.274 --> 00:03:35.913
bond.

00:03:36.154 --> 00:03:39.113
As the last video showed, alkynes decolourise it

00:03:39.194 --> 00:03:42.233
too, usually more slowly, so the test alone

00:03:42.314 --> 00:03:43.633
can't tell you which.

00:03:43.914 --> 00:03:46.153
No colour change points to no double or

00:03:46.234 --> 00:03:49.753
triple bond, even when the formula looks unsaturated:

00:03:49.794 --> 00:03:53.033
cyclohexane is C six H twelve, the same

00:03:53.114 --> 00:03:54.433
formula as hexene.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:56.953
And some other kinds of compounds can decolourise

00:03:57.034 --> 00:03:59.273
bromine water too, so treat it as a

00:03:59.354 --> 00:04:02.393
quick test: strong evidence, not proof.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:05.840
Now the variables.

00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:08.960
You change the hydrocarbon: with or without a

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:09.880
double bond.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.360
You observe the colour of both layers.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.120
Everything else stays the same: the volumes, the

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.800
bromine water, the shaking, the light and the

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:18.600
timing.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.680
Validity: the matched pair and the control mean

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.000
the result comes from the double bond, not

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:25.120
from dilution.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.520
Reliability: repeats give the same result.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:30.880
And for a yes or no test, accuracy

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.360
means getting the right answer, which you check

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:34.800
with known samples.

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.200
The risk assessment.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.880
Cyclohexane and cyclohexene are highly flammable, so no

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.000
flames anywhere near them, and their vapour shouldn't

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:48.680
be breathed, so work in a fume cupboard.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:52.000
Bromine water gives off bromine vapour, which irritates

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:54.640
the eyes and lungs, so your teacher dispenses

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.560
it in the fume cupboard, and you wear

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.400
safety glasses and gloves.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.840
The products contain bromine, so every residue goes

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.040
into the labelled organic waste container, never the

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:05.600
sink.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.840
To read the tube, remember: gone, not moved.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:14.320
If the colour has gone from both layers,

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.560
the bromine was used up, so there's a

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:17.520
multiple bond.

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.840
If it has only moved into the top

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:21.680
layer, nothing reacted.

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:24.340
Your turn.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.580
A liquid hydrocarbon, X, has the molecular formula

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:30.500
C five H ten.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.220
A student shakes an excess of X with

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:35.940
a little bromine water in normal room light.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.540
Two layers form: the top layer is orange,

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:41.620
and the bottom layer is pale yellow.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.060
Identify X as pent-one-ene or cyclopentane, and justify

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:47.700
your answer.

00:05:48.260 --> 00:05:49.620
Pause here to answer.

00:06:00.834 --> 00:06:02.873
X is cyclopentane.

00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:05.793
Both compounds are C five H ten, so

00:06:05.874 --> 00:06:07.473
the formula can't decide.

00:06:07.794 --> 00:06:09.793
X floats on the water, so the top

00:06:09.874 --> 00:06:11.073
layer is X.

00:06:11.314 --> 00:06:13.953
It's orange, so the bromine dissolved in X

00:06:14.154 --> 00:06:17.433
instead of reacting: it moved, it didn't go.

00:06:17.714 --> 00:06:19.873
An excess of pent-one-ene would have used it

00:06:19.954 --> 00:06:20.353
all up.

00:06:20.754 --> 00:06:23.393
Calling the paler bottom layer a positive result

00:06:23.554 --> 00:06:25.153
is the classic mistake.

00:06:27.167 --> 00:06:27.726
One more.

00:06:28.007 --> 00:06:31.326
In a teacher demonstration, a solution of bromine

00:06:31.367 --> 00:06:34.686
in heptane stays orange in a dark cupboard.

00:06:34.767 --> 00:06:37.966
In bright light, its colour slowly fades, and

00:06:38.047 --> 00:06:39.566
the gas at the mouth of the tube

00:06:39.647 --> 00:06:42.206
turns damp blue litmus red.

00:06:42.287 --> 00:06:45.566
Explain these observations, and write a balanced equation,

00:06:45.727 --> 00:06:48.766
using structural formulas, for the formation of one

00:06:48.847 --> 00:06:50.126
organic product.

00:06:50.447 --> 00:06:51.806
Pause here to answer.

00:07:03.034 --> 00:07:05.913
Heptane has only single bonds, so in the

00:07:06.114 --> 00:07:07.633
dark, nothing adds.

00:07:07.914 --> 00:07:11.033
Light supplies the energy to start a substitution:

00:07:11.154 --> 00:07:13.433
one hydrogen is swapped for a bromine, and

00:07:13.514 --> 00:07:16.393
the hydrogen leaves as H Br, the acidic

00:07:16.474 --> 00:07:18.553
gas that turned the litmus red.

00:07:18.594 --> 00:07:23.033
For example, heptane plus bromine gives two-bromoheptane plus

00:07:23.114 --> 00:07:24.233
H Br.

00:07:24.554 --> 00:07:27.993
Writing C seven H sixteen Br two can't

00:07:28.074 --> 00:07:30.153
be right: there's no double bond to add

00:07:30.234 --> 00:07:30.953
across.

00:07:32.967 --> 00:07:36.486
So: match the samples, add a water control,

00:07:36.567 --> 00:07:38.886
shake each tube the same way in normal

00:07:38.967 --> 00:07:41.126
light, and read both layers.

00:07:41.447 --> 00:07:44.886
Gone means a multiple bond, moved means none.

00:07:45.367 --> 00:07:47.286
And say what the test can't tell you.

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:51.846
Next: practice questions on naming, isomers and these

00:07:51.927 --> 00:07:52.726
reactions.

00:07:54.734 --> 00:07:56.173
Here are the key notes.

00:07:56.414 --> 00:07:58.333
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:58.414 --> 00:07:59.213
to copy them.
